# Line-ending policy (blueprint §3.0).
#
# Why this file exists: `core.autocrlf=true` with no `.gitattributes` means
# every tool that rewrites a file with its own newline convention — Obsidian
# re-saving a note, in the case that prompted this — shows up as a phantom
# modification with an empty diff. `git status` stops meaning anything, and a
# status you have learned to ignore is worse than no status at all.
#
# The rule: text is normalised to LF in the repository. What lands in the
# working tree is still governed by `core.autocrlf`, so Windows editors keep
# seeing CRLF; what is *stored* is LF, always, on every machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the exception, and it is not cosmetic. `cmd.exe`
# parses a `.bat` line-by-line as it executes; an LF-only file can drop the
# last character of a line inside a parenthesised block or a `goto` target and
# fail in ways that read as a corrupt script. `scripts/cargo.bat` is the only
# supported way to build this project on the dev machine, so it stays CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary: never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.dxf binary
